WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.920
How do you say this word: PECAN?
Fun fact, it’s the state nut of Alabama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.400
Arkansas, California, and Texas.
But is it puh-cawn, pee-cawn, pee-can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.600
or even puh-can? I don’t know anyone who says&nbsp;
that last one… Maybe you say it differently in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.520
different contexts…butter puh-cawn ice cream,&nbsp;
pee-can pie… Well, you’re free to say what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.200
want, but we get the name for the nut, which&nbsp;
is native to North America, from the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.080
who are native to North America! Specifically&nbsp;
from those who speak Algonquin languages, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.440
which there are around 30.
In Kickapoo, the word for nut is pakaana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.400
in Menominee it’s pakān, in Ojibwemowin bagaan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.560
in Bodewadmimwen (Potawatomi) bgan or&nbsp;
pkan, and in Miami-Illinois pakaani.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.520
And while people in the US have all sorts of&nbsp;
different pronunciations for those same five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.680
letters, it seems like the closer you are&nbsp;
to the area where the trees natively grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.160
the more likely you are to have&nbsp;
the source pronunciation: puh-cawn.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.800
So the next time you’re crackin’ nuts&nbsp;
or sittin’ down to a nice slice of pie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.880
you might take a second to think&nbsp;
about where your words came from!

